        <div id="what-12kw-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a 12,000 Watt Generator Really Means
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Machines Share the 12,000 Watt Label</h3>

          <p>
            The phrase covers three different machines. The most common is a generator with 12,000 starting watts and about 9,500 running watts, such as the DuroMax XP12000HX and Westinghouse WGen9500DF. The second is a generator with 13,000 to 13,500 starting watts and 10,500 running watts, like the DuroMax XP13000HX and Westinghouse WGen10500DFc. The third is a true 12kW: 12,000 running watts and around 15,000 starting, which in a portable means a V-twin engine like the one in the Champion 201161.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read Running Watts Before the Name</h3>

          <p>
            Those differences are not small. The gap between 9,500 and 12,000 running watts is 2,500 watts, about the running draw of a two-ton air conditioner or half an electric dryer. It also shows up in weight: the 9,500 watt machines weigh roughly 210 to 225 lb, the 10,500 watt machines 230 to 246 lb, and the true-12kW Champion 347 lb. Read the running watt figure first, and the name second.
          </p>

