        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a 12,000 watt generator run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 10,500 to 12,000 running-watt generator runs a refrigerator, freezer, furnace, 240V well pump, sump pump, lights, electronics and a soft-started central air system at once, with room for an electric water heater or dryer cycling. A 9,500 running-watt model covers the same essentials but with less room for the large 240V loads.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 12,000 watt generator run a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For many gas-heated homes it can run nearly everything, provided the central air has a soft starter and you avoid running every large 240V appliance at once. Homes with electric heat, heat pumps used for heating, or more than one big air conditioner usually need a standby generator instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 12kW generator the same as 12,000 running watts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually. Most portables sold as 12,000 watts produce 12,000 starting watts and about 9,500 running watts. The Champion 201161 is one of the few portables rated for 12,000 running watts on gasoline, and it uses a V-twin engine and weighs 347 lb.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 12,000 watt generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Based on manufacturers' ratings, about 8 to 9 hours at half load for most of this class, and up to 17 to 19 hours at quarter load on models like the DuroMax XP13000HX and Westinghouse WGen10500DFc. The Generac GP15000E's 16-gallon tank is rated for about 10 hours at half load.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a 12,000 watt generator need a 50 amp transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                To use its full output, yes. A 30-amp, 240V connection carries at most 7,200 watts, while a 50-amp connection carries up to 12,000. Use the generator's 50-amp outlet with a 50-amp inlet, a 50-amp transfer switch or interlock, and a 6-gauge cord installed by an electrician.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an 11,000 watt generator big enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many 11,000 watt generators, including the Champion 201417 and DuroMax XP11000iH, are rated for 9,000 running watts. That covers essentials plus a soft-started central air system, but leaves less room for an electric water heater or dryer at the same time than the 10,500 and 12,000 running-watt models.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a 12,000 watt portable or a standby generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose portable if outages are occasional, you want to keep costs down and you can move and fuel the generator. Choose standby if outages are frequent or long, nobody can safely handle a 250 to 350 lb generator, or you want automatic power while away. Installation often costs as much as the unit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming 12,000 Watts Means 12,000 Running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most generators sold as 12,000 watts run on 9,500. Only V-twin models like the Champion 201161 sustain a true 12,000. If your load calculation needs more than 10,000 running watts, check the running rating on the fuel you will use before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Connecting Through a 30-Amp Inlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 30-amp, 240V connection carries at most 7,200 watts, so a 9,500 to 12,000 watt generator would waste much of its output. Install a 50-amp inlet, a 50-amp transfer switch or interlock, and a 6-gauge cord, and use the generator's 14-50R outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Weight Until Delivery Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                These generators weigh 211 to 363 lb dry and more with fuel. Plan the path from storage to the run position, and consider a pad or permanent enclosure for the heaviest units. A generator you cannot move in a storm is no use when the power fails.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning on Gasoline for a Long Outage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At roughly 1 gallon an hour or more at half load, a 12kW generator can burn 15 to 25 gallons a day. Few households can store that safely. Choose dual or tri fuel, and set up a 100 lb propane cylinder or a natural gas connection if outages here last days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Huge Portable Instead of Pricing Standby</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Once you are looking at 15,000 watts, 350-plus lb and gasoline only, a standby generator with automatic start and natural gas fuel may cost less over time than it seems. Get a standby quote before buying the biggest portable you can find.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

